{
  "id": "nominative-accusative",
  "level": "A1",
  "title": "Nominativ und Akkusativ",
  "group": "Cases",
  "sourceFile": "nominakkus.html",
  "overviewSource": "nomakkexpl.html",
  "overview": {
    "blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": "German nouns change form depending on their role in the sentence. At A1 level we focus on nominative (subject) and accusative (direct object)."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "content": "Nominative — the subject"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": "The subject performs the action. Ask: \"Who or what is doing the verb?\" Nouns after sein (to be) are also nominative."
      },
      {
        "type": "example",
        "de": "Der Tisch ist braun.",
        "note": "Der Tisch = subject (nominative)"
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "content": "Accusative — the direct object"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": "The direct object receives the action. Ask: \"Who or what is being verbed?\" Only masculine articles change: der → den, ein → einen."
      },
      {
        "type": "example",
        "de": "Ich sehe den Tisch.",
        "note": "den Tisch = direct object (accusative masculine)"
      },
      {
        "type": "example",
        "de": "Er hat ein Buch.",
        "note": "Er = subject · ein Buch = object (neuter, no change)"
      },
      {
        "type": "table",
        "content": "",
        "rows": [
          [
            "",
            "Nominative",
            "Accusative"
          ],
          [
            "Masc.",
            "der / ein",
            "den / einen"
          ],
          [
            "Fem.",
            "die / eine",
            "die / eine"
          ],
          [
            "Neut.",
            "das / ein",
            "das / ein"
          ],
          [
            "Plural",
            "die / keine",
            "die / keine"
          ]
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "content": "Nominative: subjects; after sein\nAccusative: direct objects; after accusative prepositions (durch, für, ohne…)"
      }
    ]
  },
